{"kind":"ThinkAction","thought":"Now I understand the binary structure. It's an ELF64 executable with various sections. The key sections for memory extraction are:\n- .text at 0x1060 (executable code)\n- .rodata at 0x2000 (read-only data)\n- .data at 0x4000 (data section)\n- .bss at 0x4010 (uninitialized data)\n- .init_array at 0x3db8 (initialization functions)\n- .fini_array at 0x3dc0 (finalization functions)\n- .got at 0x3fb8 (global offset table)\n- .plt at 0x1020 (procedure linkage table)\n\nThe binary is 15960 bytes. I need to extract memory values as 32-bit integers (4 bytes each) from these sections. The addresses in the ELF are virtual addresses, but I need to map them to file offsets to read the actual bytes.\n\nLet me create a Node.js program that:\n1. Reads the ELF header to understand the structure\n2. Extracts data from all sections that contain actual data (PROGBITS type)\n3. Reads 4 bytes at a time and converts to 32-bit integers\n4. Outputs as JSON with address as key\n\nI'll need to use the program headers or section headers to determine which parts of the file to read."}
